    "power_work_calculation",
    "graph_sketching"
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q03_a_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": [
        "definition_recall"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Recall Newton's second law stated in terms of momentum rather than in terms of mass times acceleration."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: State force as rate of change of momentum."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Force is defined by Newton's second law in momentum form as the rate of change of momentum of a body. In symbols, $$F = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$$ where $p$ is momentum and $t$ is time."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Writing $F = ma$ only, which is a special case for constant mass and does not earn the definition mark.",
        "Confusing force with energy or pressure, for example stating force is rate of change of energy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q03_a_ii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "resultant_impulse_calculation"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Identify that change in momentum equals impulse of the resultant force, so first find the net forward force by subtracting the 0.80 N resistive force from $X$.",
        "Read the value of $X$ from Fig. 3.2 for $t = 0$ to $t = 3.0$ s and multiply the resultant force by the 3.0 s time interval."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: Resultant force times time: change in momentum = (1.4 - 0.80) x 3.0.",
        "A1: = 1.8 kg m s-1."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Impulse of the resultant force equals change in momentum: $$\\Delta p = F_{net} \\Delta t$$ where $F_{net} = X - F_{resistive}$.",
        "From Fig. 3.2, for $t = 0$ to $t = 3.0$ s, $X = 1.4$ N. The resistive force is $0.80$ N, so $$F_{net} = 1.4 - 0.80 = 0.60 \\text{ N}$$",
        "Multiply by time interval $\\Delta t = 3.0$ s: $$\\Delta p = 0.60 \\times 3.0 = 1.8 \\text{ kg m s}^{-1}$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using $X$ alone (1.4 N) without subtracting the resistive force, giving $4.2$ kg m s-1.",
        "Misreading $X$ from the graph as 1.5 N or 1.3 N instead of carefully counting grid squares to 1.4 N."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q03_b_i",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "newton_first_law_application"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Read the value of $X$ from Fig. 3.2 between $t = 3.0$ s and $t = 6.0$ s and compare it with the 0.80 N resistive force to find the resultant force.",
        "Apply Newton's first law to link zero resultant force to the velocity of the block."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: Resultant force on block is zero (X = 0.80 N balances resistive force).",
        "B1: So velocity is constant (uniform motion, zero acceleration)."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "From Fig. 3.2, for $t = 3.0$ s to $t = 6.0$ s, $X = 0.80$ N. The resistive force is $0.80$ N in the opposite direction, so $$F_{net} = 0.80 - 0.80 = 0 \\text{ N}$$",
        "By Newton's first law, with zero resultant force there is zero acceleration. The block is already moving, so it continues to move with constant velocity."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating the block stops because forces are balanced, forgetting that balanced forces mean constant velocity, not zero velocity.",
        "Stating constant speed without explaining that resultant force is zero, which loses the explanation mark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q03_b_ii",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "question_patterns": [
        "power_work_calculation"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Recall the power relation for a force moving at constant velocity: $P = Fv$, where $F$ here is force $X$ (0.80 N) and $P = 2.0$ W.",
        "Rearrange to find the constant velocity $v$ from $v = P / F$ using $P = 2.0$ W and $F = 0.80$ N.",
        "Use $distance = velocity \\times time$ with time interval $6.0 - 3.0 = 3.0$ s to find the distance moved."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: P = Fv or P = Fs / t.",
        "C1: v = 2.0 / 0.80 (= 2.5 m s-1).",
        "A1: distance = 2.5 x 3.0 = 7.5 m."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Power delivered by a force at constant velocity is $$P = Fv$$ where $F$ is the driving force $X$ and $v$ is velocity.",
        "Between $t = 3.0$ s and $t = 6.0$ s, $P = 2.0$ W and $F = X = 0.80$ N, so $$v = \\frac{P}{F} = \\frac{2.0}{0.80}$$ $$v = 2.5 \\text{ m s}^{-1}$$",
        "Time interval is $$\\Delta t = 6.0 - 3.0 = 3.0 \\text{ s}$$ Distance at constant velocity is $$s = v \\Delta t = 2.5 \\times 3.0 = 7.5 \\text{ m}$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using the resultant force (0 N) in $P = Fv$ instead of the driving force $X = 0.80$ N.",
        "Using the wrong time interval, for example 6.0 s instead of 3.0 s, giving double the distance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q03_c",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "graph_sketching"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Link gradient of momentum time graph to resultant force: constant positive resultant force from $t = 0$ to $t = 3.0$ s gives a straight line of constant positive gradient starting at zero.",
        "Link zero resultant force from $t = 3.0$ s to $t = 6.0$ s to zero gradient, so momentum stays constant with no sudden jump at $t = 3.0$ s."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: 0 to 3.0 s: upward sloping straight line from the origin.",
        "B1: 3.0 to 6.0 s: horizontal line at non-zero value with no step change at 3.0 s."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Gradient of a momentum time graph equals resultant force since $F_{net} = \\Delta p / \\Delta t$.",
        "From $t = 0$ to $t = 3.0$ s, $F_{net} = 0.60$ N is constant and positive, and initial momentum is zero because the block is at rest. So draw a straight line of constant positive gradient starting at the origin.",
        "From $t = 3.0$ s to $t = 6.0$ s, $F_{net} = 0$, so $\\Delta p = 0$. Draw a horizontal line continuing smoothly from the value reached at $t = 3.0$ s, with no vertical step."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Drawing a curved momentum graph, forgetting that constant force gives constant rate of gain of momentum (straight line).",
        "Drawing a drop or step down at $t = 3.0$ s when $X$ drops, whereas momentum cannot change instantaneously when force changes."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question_difficulty": 2,
  "question_patterns": [
    "property_identification",
    "direct_calculation",
    "standing_wave_interpretation",
    "polarisation_malus_law"
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_a_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["property_identification"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall the definition of a transverse wave in terms of the direction of particle vibration P relative to the direction of energy propagation Q."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: For transverse wave, vibration perpendicular to propagation, so P perpendicular to Q."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "The defining property of a transverse wave is that the particle vibration is at right angles to the direction of energy transfer. Here $P$ is the vibration direction and $Q$ is the propagation direction, so for a transverse wave $P$ is perpendicular to $Q$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Confusing transverse with longitudinal and stating parallel.",
        "Describing P and Q as opposite rather than stating the angle between them."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_a_ii",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["property_identification"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall the definition of a longitudinal wave in terms of the direction of particle vibration P relative to the direction of energy propagation Q."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: For longitudinal wave, vibration parallel to propagation, so P parallel to Q (along same line)."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "The defining property of a longitudinal wave is that the particle vibration is along the same line as the direction of energy transfer. Here $P$ is the vibration direction and $Q$ is the propagation direction, so for a longitudinal wave $P$ is parallel to $Q$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating perpendicular by reversing the two wave types.",
        "Saying the lines are the same point rather than parallel lines."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_b_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["direct_calculation"],
      "hints": [
        "Identify the wave equation linking speed $v$, frequency $f$ and wavelength $\\lambda$ for sound.",
        "Convert the frequency from kHz to Hz before substituting $v$ and $f$ into the rearranged equation $\\lambda = v/f$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: Use lambda = v / f.",
        "A1: = 340 / 1700 = 0.20 m."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "The wave speed is governed by $$v = f \\lambda$$ Rearranged for wavelength: $$\\lambda = \\frac{v}{f}$$ Convert frequency: $$f = 1.7 \\text{ kHz} = 1.7 \\times 10^3 \\text{ Hz} = 1700 \\text{ Hz}$$ Substitute: $$\\lambda = \\frac{340}{1700}$$ Evaluate: $$\\lambda = 0.20 \\text{ m}$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Forgetting to convert 1.7 kHz to 1700 Hz, giving a factor of 1000 error.",
        "Inverting the formula to lambda = f / v."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_b_ii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": ["standing_wave_interpretation"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall that a closed end must be a displacement node and the open end is an antinode A, then count how many quarter wavelengths fit between the open antinode and the closed node using Fig. 5.1 with two antinodes."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: L = 3/4 x lambda = 3/4 x 0.20 = 0.15 m."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "For a tube closed at one end, the closed end is a node and the open end is an antinode. The separation between an adjacent antinode and node is $$\\frac{\\lambda}{4}$$ From Fig. 5.1, going from the open end to the closed end the pattern is antinode to node to antinode to node. That is three quarter wavelength sections: $$L = 3 \\times \\frac{\\lambda}{4} = \\frac{3\\lambda}{4}$$ Substitute $\\lambda = 0.20 \\text{ m}$: $$L = 0.75 \\times 0.20$$ $$L = 0.15 \\text{ m}$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Assuming L equals one full wavelength or one half wavelength instead of counting nodes and antinodes.",
        "Confusing open and closed end conditions and using L = lambda/2."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_b_iii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": ["standing_wave_interpretation"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall the fundamental (lowest frequency, longest wavelength) mode of a closed tube has only one antinode at the open end and one node at the closed end, so L equals one quarter wavelength."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: lambda_max = 4 x L = 4 x 0.15 = 0.60 m."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "The longest possible stationary wavelength corresponds to the fundamental mode. For a tube closed at one end: $$L = \\frac{\\lambda_{max}}{4}$$ Rearranged: $$\\lambda_{max} = 4L$$ Substitute $L = 0.15 \\text{ m}$: $$\\lambda_{max} = 4 \\times 0.15$$ $$\\lambda_{max} = 0.60 \\text{ m}$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using the open tube fundamental L = lambda/2 instead of L = lambda/4.",
        "Thinking the given mode with two antinodes is already the longest wavelength."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_c_i",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": ["polarisation_malus_law"],
      "hints": [
        "Apply Malus law for polarised light: transmitted intensity equals incident intensity times cos squared of the angle between the incident polarisation direction and the transmission axis."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: I = 8.5 x cos^2 35 deg = 5.7 W m^-2."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Malus law for already polarised light incident on a polariser is $$I = I_0 \\cos^2 \\theta$$ where $\\theta$ is the angle between the incident polarisation and the transmission axis. Here $I_0 = 8.5 \\text{ W m}^{-2}$ and $\\theta = 35^{\\circ}$: $$I = 8.5 \\times \\cos^2(35^{\\circ})$$ Evaluate $\\cos(35^{\\circ})$: $$\\cos(35^{\\circ}) = 0.8192$$ Square it: $$\\cos^2(35^{\\circ}) = 0.8192^2 = 0.6710$$ Multiply: $$I = 8.5 \\times 0.6710 = 5.70 \\text{ W m}^{-2}$$ This shows the transmitted intensity is $5.7 \\text{ W m}^{-2}$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using cos instead of cos squared, or using Malus law for unpolarised light I0/2.",
        "Calculator in radian mode when evaluating cos 35 degrees."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q05_c_ii",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "question_patterns": ["polarisation_malus_law", "algebraic_derivation"],
      "hints": [
        "Identify that the light incident on the second filter has intensity $5.7 \\text{ W m}^{-2}$ and is polarised along the first filter axis, so the relevant angle in Malus law is the angle between the two transmission axes.",
        "Set up $5.2 = 5.7 \\cos^2(\\alpha - 35^{\\circ})$ and rearrange to find $\\cos(\\alpha - 35^{\\circ})$ by dividing then square rooting."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: 5.2 = 5.7 cos^2 theta, giving theta = alpha - 35 deg = 17 deg.",
        "A1: alpha = 35 + 17 = 52 deg."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Apply Malus law at the second filter. Incident intensity $I_0 = 5.7 \\text{ W m}^{-2}$, transmitted $I = 5.2 \\text{ W m}^{-2}$: $$I = I_0 \\cos^2 \\phi$$ where $\\phi$ is the angle between the two transmission axes, $\\phi = \\alpha - 35^{\\circ}$. So: $$5.2 = 5.7 \\times \\cos^2 \\phi$$ Divide: $$\\cos^2 \\phi = \\frac{5.2}{5.7} = 0.9123$$ Square root: $$\\cos \\phi = \\sqrt{0.9123} = 0.9551$$ Inverse cosine: $$\\phi = \\cos^{-1}(0.9551) = 17.2^{\\circ}$$ Hence: $$\\alpha = 35^{\\circ} + \\phi = 35^{\\circ} + 17.2^{\\circ} = 52^{\\circ}$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using 35 degrees directly as the angle for the second filter instead of the difference alpha minus 35 degrees.",
        "Forgetting to square root after dividing, or forgetting to add the 35 degrees back to get alpha."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question_difficulty": 2,
  "question_patterns": [
    "property_identification",
    "direct_calculation",
    "algebraic_derivation"
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_a",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["property_identification"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall that current in a filament causes heating, so consider how filament temperature changes when current decreases and how resistance of a metal depends on temperature due to lattice ion vibration and electron collisions."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: Temperature of filament decreases as heating effect is reduced, so resistance decreases."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Resistance of a metal filament depends on temperature. Current produces a heating effect with power $P = I^2 R$, so a smaller current gives less heating and the filament temperature falls. In a metal, higher temperature means greater amplitude of vibration of lattice ions and more frequent collisions with conduction electrons, giving higher resistance. Therefore when temperature falls, collision rate falls and resistance decreases."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating resistance increases because lower current is assumed to mean higher resistance from $R = V/I$ without considering that $V$ also changes and that filament is non-ohmic.",
        "Omitting the temperature link and only stating resistance decreases, which does not earn the explanation mark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_b_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": ["direct_calculation"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall the definition of resistance linking potential difference $V$ and current, and rearrange to make current the subject in terms of $V$ and $R$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: current = V / R from $R = V/I$."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Resistance is defined by $$R = \\frac{V}{I}$$ where $V$ is potential difference across the wire and $I$ is current. Rearranging to make current the subject: $$I = \\frac{V}{R}$$ This is the current in terms of $V$ and $R$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Inverting the relation to give $IR/V$ or $VR$, confusing subject of $R = V/I$.",
        "Introducing $N$, $e$ or $L$ unnecessarily in this part, although only $V$ and $R$ are needed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_b_ii",
      "difficulty": 3,
      "question_patterns": ["algebraic_derivation"],
      "hints": [
        "Start from the drift speed relation $I = nAve$ where $n$ is number density of free electrons, $A$ is cross-sectional area and $v$ is drift speed, and identify that $n$ must be found from total number $N$ divided by volume $AL$.",
        "Substitute $n = N/(AL)$ into $v = I/(nAe)$ and then substitute $I = V/R$ from (b)(i), then cancel $A$ to leave $v$ in terms of $V$, $L$, $R$, $N$ and $e$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: Use $I = nAve$ with $n = N/(AL)$ where $AL$ is volume.",
        "A1: $v = I/(nAe) = (V/R)/[(N/AL)e] = VL/(RNe)$."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Current is related to drift speed by $$I = nAv e$$ where $n$ is number of free electrons per unit volume, $A$ is cross-sectional area, $v$ is average drift speed. Rearranged: $$v = \\frac{I}{nAe}$$ Number density $n$ is total number divided by volume. Volume of cylindrical wire is area times length: $$\\text{volume} = AL$$ so $$n = \\frac{N}{AL}$$ Substitute $n$ and $I = V/R$ from (b)(i): $$v = \\frac{V/R}{\\left(N/AL\\right)Ae}$$ The area $A$ cancels: $$v = \\frac{V/R}{Ne/L}$$ $$v = \\frac{VL}{RNe}$$ with units of $\\text{m s}^{-1}$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using $N$ directly as number density $n$, forgetting to divide by volume $AL$, leading to missing factor $L$.",
        "Failing to cancel cross-sectional area $A$ correctly or introducing unknown $A$ into final expression when question requires only $e, L, N, R, V$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q06_b_iii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": ["algebraic_derivation"],
      "hints": [
        "Recall that average time to travel length $L$ at constant drift speed $v$ is $t = L/v$, or equivalently total charge $Ne$ divided by current $I$, then substitute $v$ from (b)(ii) or $I$ from (b)(i)."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "A1: $t = L/v = L/(VL/RNe) = RNe/V$, or $t = Q/I = Ne/(V/R) = RNe/V$."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Time for uniform motion is distance divided by speed: $$t = \\frac{L}{v}$$ Substitute $v = VL/(RNe)$ from (b)(ii): $$t = \\frac{L}{VL/(RNe)}$$ Invert denominator: $$t = \\frac{L RNe}{VL}$$ Cancel $L$: $$t = \\frac{RNe}{V}$$ Equivalently, total charge passing is $Q = Ne$ and $t = Q/I = Ne/(V/R)$ gives the same result, with units of $\\text{s}$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Inverting $t = L/v$ to $v/L$, giving $V/(RNe)$ instead of $RNe/V$.",
        "Confusing total charge $Ne$ with elementary charge $e$ alone when using $t = Q/I$."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question_difficulty": 2,
  "question_patterns": [
    "property_identification",
    "particle_decay_conservation"
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_a_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": [
        "property_identification"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Recall the definition of isotopes in terms of proton number Z and nucleon number A for the same element.",
        "Hint 2: Deduce what must be identical (determined by Z) and what must differ to give different A for P and Q."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: Isotopes have same proton number, so number of protons is the same/equal.",
        "B1: Isotopes have different nucleon numbers, so number of neutrons is different/unequal."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Isotopes are nuclei of the same element with the same proton number $Z$ but different nucleon number $A$.",
        "Since $Z$ is the same, the number of protons is equal for P and Q.",
        "Since $A = Z + N$ differs, with $Z$ constant, the neutron number $N = A - Z$ must be different for P and Q."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Reversing the comparison, stating protons differ and neutrons are the same.",
        "Describing isotopes only as having different masses without specifying protons versus neutrons."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_a_ii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "property_identification"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Recall that $\\beta^-$ decay is a neutron changing to a proton inside the nucleus, and write the quark make-up of a neutron (udd) and a proton (uud) to see which quark flavour changes."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: A down quark changes to an up quark (udd changes to uud)."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "In $\\beta^-$ decay a neutron converts to a proton.",
        "Neutron quark composition is up down down: $$udd$$",
        "Proton quark composition is up up down: $$uud$$",
        "Therefore one down quark changes to an up quark."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating up changes to down, which is the change for $\\beta^+$ decay, not $\\beta^-$ decay.",
        "Describing only nucleon change (neutron to proton) without giving the required quark level change."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_a_iii",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": [
        "particle_decay_conservation"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Apply conservation of lepton number and energy in $\\beta^-$ decay and recall which nearly massless neutral lepton is emitted with the electron."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: (Electron) antineutrino is also emitted."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Conservation of lepton number, energy and momentum in $\\beta^-$ decay requires a second lepton in addition to the electron.",
        "This particle is the (electron) antineutrino, denoted $\\bar{\\nu}_e$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating neutrino instead of antineutrino, which would violate lepton number conservation.",
        "Stating gamma photon, confusing beta decay with gamma de-excitation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_b",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "direct_calculation"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Recall the quark charges in units of $e$: charm quark is $+2/3e$ and bottom quark is $-1/3e$.",
        "Hint 2: Add the three quark charges together for two charm quarks plus one bottom quark to find the total hadron charge."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: Charge of charm = +2/3e (so 2 charm = +4/3e) and charge of bottom = -1/3e.",
        "A1: Total = +2/3e + 2/3e - 1/3e = +1e."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Total charge is the algebraic sum of the constituent quark charges.",
        "Quark charges in terms of $e$: $$Q_{charm} = +\\frac{2}{3}e$$ $$Q_{bottom} = -\\frac{1}{3}e$$",
        "For two charm quarks and one bottom quark: $$Q_{total} = Q_{charm} + Q_{charm} + Q_{bottom}$$",
        "Substitute values: $$Q_{total} = \\left(+\\frac{2}{3}e\\right) + \\left(+\\frac{2}{3}e\\right) + \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}e\\right)$$",
        "Combine fractions: $$Q_{total} = \\frac{2+2-1}{3}e = \\frac{3}{3}e = +1e$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using wrong sign for bottom quark, taking it as +1/3e instead of -1/3e.",
        "Forgetting there are two charm quarks and adding only one charm charge to one bottom charge."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
